[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186375288 :answer-field/choices 17592186375289] [:db/retract 17592186375288 :answer-field/choices 17592186375290] [:db/retract 17592186375288 :answer-field/choices 17592186375291] [:db/retract 17592186375288 :answer-field/choices 17592186375292] [:db/retract 17592186375288 :answer-field/choices 17592186375293] [:db/retract 17592186129840 :question/answer-fields 17592186128521] [:db/retract 17592186341458 :answer-field/choices 17592186341299] [:db/retract 17592186341458 :answer-field/choices 17592186340197] [:db/retract 17592186341458 :answer-field/choices 17592186339386] [:db/retract 17592186341458 :answer-field/choices 17592186340125] [:db/retract 17592186341458 :answer-field/choices 17592186339183] {:db/id 17592186129840 :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=4.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/c5/c55d5f809462b767122ccc4af97c5b4d243730ed24f655bdc18e35ec57e25ce2.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=\\frac{y}{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1.$ So, we have $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375288 :answer-field/correct "answer-f038c740-7bd9-542c-aa85-3c158363caa4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98af0664-fef3-5195-8942-4ae62f79f459" "answer-86e11a1f-00dc-51de-8fae-8e31288e9455" "answer-f038c740-7bd9-542c-aa85-3c158363caa4" "answer-a5a1af78-5337-58fe-9506-fb9b3db058f1" "answer-9f3d9aa4-b230-5307-8b9e-8fda1b89d36a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98af0664-fef3-5195-8942-4ae62f79f459" :answer/id #uuid "98af0664-fef3-5195-8942-4ae62f79f459" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86e11a1f-00dc-51de-8fae-8e31288e9455" :answer/id #uuid "86e11a1f-00dc-51de-8fae-8e31288e9455" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f038c740-7bd9-542c-aa85-3c158363caa4" :answer/id #uuid "f038c740-7bd9-542c-aa85-3c158363caa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5a1af78-5337-58fe-9506-fb9b3db058f1" :answer/id #uuid "a5a1af78-5337-58fe-9506-fb9b3db058f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 2\\,,\\,2x\\le y\\le (x-1)^{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3d9aa4-b230-5307-8b9e-8fda1b89d36a" :answer/id #uuid "9f3d9aa4-b230-5307-8b9e-8fda1b89d36a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 3\\,,\\,2x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375321 :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/12/1206abdf317df09c8c54714a6178d78570b3c10a49513c3c626c09be3ea2cf72.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186342005 :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-2(x-3)\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/fb/fb05eec73599c1beb8dd588c478208315f6edaf10af999115eb29fa10d5601e9.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ and leaves through the upper function $x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186341458 :answer-field/correct "answer-a8039587-077a-5341-9003-3971e08c6ca6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b4fa72f4-3cd9-588c-8d31-a887a4ecafd6" "answer-f6ff3e83-5d71-5daa-b2f8-25c47f45a202" "answer-a8039587-077a-5341-9003-3971e08c6ca6" "answer-1756c48c-665f-5f06-b577-1b909cc87f92" "answer-1a8d8801-ef97-55e2-bc3a-31cf1be29d3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4fa72f4-3cd9-588c-8d31-a887a4ecafd6" :answer/id #uuid "b4fa72f4-3cd9-588c-8d31-a887a4ecafd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le y\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6ff3e83-5d71-5daa-b2